@@s001 Code: graphs as adjacency lists
@@s002 Code: graphs as adjacency lists
@@s003 How to build a graph in Java, how to compute the degree of a node, how to check an edge. It is the basic code on which BFS, DFS and topological sort rest.
@@s004 Reading the graph
@@s005 The graph arrives as text: first line <code>n m</code>, then <code>m</code> lines with the edges. I store it as an adjacency list: a list of neighbours for each node.
@@s006 Reading the graph
@@s007 <code>BufferedReader</code> reads the input one line at a time. It is faster than <code>Scanner</code> on large inputs.
@@s008 I read <code>n</code> (nodes) from the first line. <code>Integer.parseInt</code> turns the text into an <code>int</code>.
@@s009 A list of lists. <code>adj.get(u)</code> is the list of neighbours of <code>u</code>. The generics <code>&lt;…&gt;</code> state the type of the elements.
@@s010 I add <code>v</code> to the neighbours of <code>u</code>.
@@s011 The graph is undirected: the edge counts in both directions, so I also add <code>u</code> to the neighbours of <code>v</code>.
@@s012 Syntax notes
@@s013 <code>import java.io.*;</code> and <code>import java.util.*;</code> import all the classes of the two packages.
@@s014 <code>throws IOException</code>: <code>readLine()</code> can fail, and Java requires you to declare it (a <i>checked</i> exception).
@@s015 <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()</code>: the diamond <code>&lt;&gt;</code> tells Java to infer the type from the context.
@@s016 Degree and the edge question
@@s017 The degree of <code>u</code> is the size of its list. Checking the edge <code>u–v</code> means searching for <code>v</code> in the list of <code>u</code>: it costs the degree of <code>u</code>.
@@s018 Degree and the edge question
@@s019 The degree is the <code>size()</code> of the list. Cost <code>O(1)</code>, because the list already knows its length.
@@s020 <code>contains</code> scans the list: cost <code>O(degree(u))</code>. With an adjacency matrix it would be <code>O(1)</code>, but at the space cost <code>O(n²)</code>.
@@s021 Syntax notes
@@s022 <code>.size()</code> is the number of elements of a list; <code>.length</code> is only for arrays.
@@s023 <code>int u = 0, v = 3;</code> declares several variables of the same type on one line.
@@s024 Running it
@@s025 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
